/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  Component: isomerA  -- SYNTHETIC witness species, carried by this case only
  (userOp03_case_rate_law).  The SUBSTRATE of the isomerisation
  isomerA -> isomerB that the case's Michaelis-Menten law drives.

  NOT A REAL SUBSTANCE -- a stand-in for a kinetics witness (CAS 00-00-0),
  like the catalogue's compA/compB/compC, and declared so below
  (`provenance { source synthetic; }`) so the engine says it on every run.

  WHY THE CASE CARRIES ITS OWN PAIR (2026-10-25).  The witness used to react
  the catalogue's compA (MW 30) into compB (MW 50) one-for-one, so each CSTR
  turned 30 kg/h into 40 kg/h and the plant's mass balance closed at
  133.333 % -- the engine reported it, correctly; the case was wrong.  compB
  is a VLLE-audit partner, never a product of compA, and the catalogue
  records are not this case's to edit.  An isomer has the substrate's molar
  mass by definition, so the reaction conserves mass with the stoichiometry
  the law is written for (nu -1/+1).

  Every value below is compA's, so the substrate's behaviour is unchanged:
  the pure-A feed is still 12.5 kmol/m3 on the liquid basis (1/Vliq).

  formula X: fictitious, and the SAME for both isomers because they are
  isomers.  X is no element, so the element balance is UNAVAILABLE (said on
  every run), as it is for every synthetic species -- it has no atoms to
  count, and inventing some would convert "no elements" into false ones.
  No standardThermochemistry block, ON PURPOSE (compA's reason): a
  fictitious species has no heat of formation.  The reactors run
  isothermal, and the energy balance is UNAVAILABLE and says so.

  Sources: none -- author-set for this witness; no physical primary exists.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

name        isomerA;
formula     X;              // fictitious; isomerB carries the same formula
CAS         00-00-0;

MW          30.0;           // kg/kmol -- EQUAL to isomerB's: an isomerisation conserves mass
Tc          500.0;
Pc          50.0;
omega       0.10;
Tb          320.0;
HvapTb      28000;
Vliq        8.0e-5;         // m3/mol -- EQUAL to isomerB's: the liquid's volumetric flow
                            // does not change with conversion, so the CSTR's
                            // c_A = c_A0 (1 - X) is exact, not an approximation

vaporPressure
{
    model         Antoine;
    coefficients  (4.0   1700.0   -30.0);   // log10 P_bar = A - B/(T + C); Psat(350 K) = 0.049 bar
    Trange        (300  400);
}

idealGasHeatCapacity
{
    model         polynomial;
    coefficients  (80.0   0.0   0.0   0.0);
    Trange        (200  500);
}

liquidHeatCapacity
{
    model         polynomial;
    coefficients  (120.0);
    Trange        (300  400);
}

provenance
{
    source      synthetic;
    reason      "the substrate of the userOp03 isomerisation witness; no physical primary exists";
}

reviewStatus interim;       // author-set witness record, not read back by a curator
